<Item XName="Page" Type="PageMgr" />
<Item XName="ReadMe" UserName="ReadMe" Type="Notes">
<Description>Example text with pretty printing</Description>
<LibName>Eagleware</LibName>
<Selected><T>Bool</T><D>1</D></Selected>
<Text>&lt;P class=MsoTitle style=&quot;BORDER-TOP: medium none; BORDER-RIGHT: medium none; BORDER-BOTTOM: windowtext 0.75pt solid; PADDING-BOTTOM: 1pt; PADDING-TOP: 0in; PADDING-LEFT: 0in; BORDER-LEFT: medium none; MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt; PADDING-RIGHT: 0in&quot; align=center&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT size=5 face=Arial&gt;Block Up Converter Spur Analysis&amp;nbsp;Workspace&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;TEXT-ALIGN: center; MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; align=center&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt; FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;EM&gt;Keysight Technologies Example Workspace&lt;/EM&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;ABSTRACT&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;This&amp;nbsp;example illustrates a swept spectrum graph on a 2 to 10 GHz block up converter. The&amp;nbsp;results are displayed similar to&amp;nbsp;WhatIF instead of the&amp;nbsp;spectrum comb that is traditionally seen.&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;BACKGROUND&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;WhatIF is a frequency planning tool used to help locate spurious free regions or analyze spurious performance for a single agile conversion stage. The graphical output is unique since each spurious product is represented as a horizontal bar whose&amp;nbsp;length tracks the spur&amp;nbsp;across the entire frequency range of the system. The height of&amp;nbsp;these bars on the graph represents the amplitudes of the spurs.&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;The upside is that WhatIF is extremely fast,&amp;nbsp;has small workspaces, can analyze the performance of every IF both in up and down conversion&amp;nbsp;cases. The downside is that it only handles a single agile mixer stage. To overcome this limitation and help users visualize spur performance of arbitrary topologies a &apos;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT color=#0000ff&gt;sweepplot()&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&apos; function has been created.&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;This &apos;sweepplot()&apos; function points to sweep spectrum data. The swept data is resorted and organized in a fashion where&amp;nbsp;traces are created from each piece of spectrum. These pieces of spectrums are tracked across the sweep range and then plotted as a frequency response bar. The height of the bar corresponds to the amplitude of the spur at that particular sweep frequency.&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;SETUP&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;There are three sections in this workspace. Each is stored in&amp;nbsp;its own folder. Spurious analysis will take place up to 5th order. It is assumed there are 6 RF blocks each having a 250 MHz bandwidth for a total of 1500 MHz.&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;1) A WhatIF analysis of a 2 to 10 GHz block up-converter. The default configuration is using the summing process between the IF input and mixer LO.&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;2) A Spectrasys sweep&amp;nbsp;that contains a single mixer was created to compare the &apos;sweepplot()&apos; to&amp;nbsp;the plots in WhatIF.&amp;nbsp; &lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;3) A complete block up converter with front end IF filter and variable gain control. Also included are some post mixer filtering along with amplification and coupling.&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;A variable called &lt;STRONG&gt;&apos;LOInjection&apos;&lt;/STRONG&gt; was created to compare a &lt;STRONG&gt;&apos;IF + LO = RF&apos;&lt;/STRONG&gt; and a &lt;STRONG&gt;&apos;LO - IF = RF&apos;&lt;/STRONG&gt; response. A &lt;STRONG&gt;&apos;0&apos;&lt;/STRONG&gt; corresponds to the &lt;STRONG&gt;sum&lt;/STRONG&gt; configuration and a &lt;STRONG&gt;&apos;1&apos;&lt;/STRONG&gt; the high side LO configuration.&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;ANALYSIS&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;EM&gt;&lt;U&gt;FreqPlan1 Graph&lt;/U&gt;&lt;/EM&gt; - This graph is an analysis of the block up-converter created by WhatIF. To toggle between the sum and difference configurations change the &lt;STRONG&gt;&apos;Desired Output Frequency&apos;&lt;/STRONG&gt; on the &lt;STRONG&gt;&apos;Inputs&apos;&lt;/STRONG&gt; tab from &lt;STRONG&gt;&apos;Sum&apos;&lt;/STRONG&gt; to &lt;STRONG&gt;&apos;Difference Show Hi Side LO, LO &amp;gt; RF&apos;&lt;/STRONG&gt;. Notice an in-band &lt;STRONG&gt;&apos;3 x 2&apos;&lt;/STRONG&gt; response exists in the &lt;STRONG&gt;&apos;Sum&apos;&lt;/STRONG&gt; configuration.&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;EM&gt;&lt;U&gt;Sweep Plot&lt;/U&gt;&lt;/EM&gt; - This graph is similar to the &lt;STRONG&gt;&apos;FreqPlan1 Graph&apos;&lt;/STRONG&gt; created by WhatIF except that this uses the &lt;STRONG&gt;&apos;sweepplot()&apos;&lt;/STRONG&gt; function. &lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT color=#0000ff&gt;NOTE: When comparing graphs between WhatIF and the &apos;sweepplot()&apos;&amp;nbsp;the spurious bandwidths are slightly different because the &apos;sweepplot()&apos; function only tracks the center of the spectrum and doesn&apos;t take into consideration the bandwidth of the carrier. Also, WhatIF graphs are displayed in &apos;dBc&apos;. The &apos;0 dBc&apos; reference point in this example = - 20 dBm.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;EM&gt;&lt;U&gt;Full Tx Sweep Plot&lt;/U&gt;&lt;/EM&gt; - This graph shows the true spurious response due to&amp;nbsp;the entire block up-converter. This graph can be compared with the &apos;FreqPlan1 Graph&apos; and &apos;Sweep Plot&apos; graphs to see the effects the additional stages have on the spurious responses.&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;EXERCISES&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;1.&amp;nbsp;Which mixer configuration (sum or difference, high side LO) has the best in-band spurious performance?&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;2. For the &lt;STRONG&gt;&apos;Full Tx Sweep Plot&apos;&lt;/STRONG&gt; who creates the positive sloped spurs located between 14 and 20 GHz?&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;3. Tune the variable gain amplifier bias and notice the affects of the intermod spectrum.&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;ANSWERS TO EXERCISES&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;1. The high side LO in a difference configuration gives better in-band spurious performance.&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;2. These positive sloped spurs are created by the &lt;STRONG&gt;&apos;TxAmp&apos;.&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;</Text>
